<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Laws</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1542">1542</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as New Laws of 1542</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The New Laws were a set of regulations issued by the Spanish crown in 1542 to govern the treatment of indigenous peoples in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a> and debates over the legitimacy of conquest, they sought to limit the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system and prohibit indigenous enslavement. Fierce resistance from colonists meant they were enforced with mixed success in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-laws.jpg" alt="New Laws" width="269" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan de Brocar (impresor), Jean de Vingles (grabador) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Leyes_Nuevas1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
